WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.497 --> 00:00:07.497
"We went by bicycle,
no automobile available

00:00:08.227 --> 00:00:10.773
"because of wartime
restrictions on their use.

00:00:12.847 --> 00:00:15.577
"On the way home, my
condition began to assume

00:00:15.577 --> 00:00:16.953
"threatening forms.

00:00:19.837 --> 00:00:23.230
"Everything in my field of
vision wavered and was distorted

00:00:25.147 --> 00:00:26.943
"as I've seen in a curved mirror.

00:00:33.697 --> 00:00:35.167
"I also had the sensation

00:00:35.167 --> 00:00:37.563
"of being unable to move from the spot.

00:00:40.477 --> 00:00:43.297
"Nevertheless, my assistant later told me

00:00:43.297 --> 00:00:45.597
"that we had traveled very rapidly."

00:00:47.370 --> 00:00:52.353
From the diary of Albert
Hoffman, Swiss chemist, 1943.

00:01:04.320 --> 00:01:06.330
Switzerland has long been obsessed

00:01:06.330 --> 00:01:07.863
with the science of the mind.

00:01:09.150 --> 00:01:10.263
Take Albert Hoffman.

00:01:11.250 --> 00:01:13.923
He was the first person to synthesize LSD.

00:01:15.120 --> 00:01:19.440
On April 19th, 1943, he
ingested a tiny amount,

00:01:19.440 --> 00:01:20.760
hopped on his bike

00:01:20.760 --> 00:01:23.610
and then realized that all
matter is merely energy

00:01:23.610 --> 00:01:25.980
condensed to a slow vibration,

00:01:25.980 --> 00:01:28.020
there is no such thing as death,

00:01:28.020 --> 00:01:30.693
and we are the imagination of ourselves.

00:01:34.140 --> 00:01:36.270
Today Swiss labs and medical companies

00:01:36.270 --> 00:01:37.740
are still pushing the frontiers

00:01:37.740 --> 00:01:39.453
of our understanding of the mind.

00:01:40.560 --> 00:01:42.780
There's less blotter involved,

00:01:42.780 --> 00:01:46.740
but they're very much turned
on and tuned in to a unique mix

00:01:46.740 --> 00:01:49.413
of neuroscience, computers and AI.

00:01:50.880 --> 00:01:53.040
The research can be dazzling.

00:01:53.040 --> 00:01:55.890
It can sometimes feel like
you need an IQ of 1000

00:01:55.890 --> 00:01:56.853
to understand it,

00:01:58.200 --> 00:02:00.630
but more and more I'm seeing

00:02:00.630 --> 00:02:02.310
that we're on the cusp of things

00:02:02.310 --> 00:02:04.503
that used to be called miracles.

00:02:08.021 --> 00:02:09.120
And now you're going to
turn on the stimulation

00:02:09.120 --> 00:02:11.163
and he'll do a leg extension.

00:02:14.970 --> 00:02:16.623
And up, the stim is on now.

00:02:17.916 --> 00:02:20.250
How does it feel when
it turns on like that?

00:02:20.250 --> 00:02:22.443
It's fantastic.

00:02:25.350 --> 00:02:27.870
Six years ago, Michele
here was in an accident

00:02:27.870 --> 00:02:30.633
that left him unable to
move his legs at all.

00:02:33.960 --> 00:02:36.543
Then he had electrodes
implanted on his spine,

00:02:38.010 --> 00:02:40.323
and well, check him out.

00:02:48.146 --> 00:02:50.250
He's one of the first
patients treated by Onward,

00:02:50.250 --> 00:02:51.840
which has created a technology

00:02:51.840 --> 00:02:54.690
to help reconnect
paralyzed people's brains

00:02:54.690 --> 00:02:55.743
with their bodies.

00:02:59.040 --> 00:03:01.050
The idea comes from a brain surgeon

00:03:01.050 --> 00:03:05.643
and a neuroscientist Jocelyne
Bloch and Grégoire Courtine.

00:03:06.630 --> 00:03:09.630
Several years ago, Grégoire
was studying paralyzed rats

00:03:09.630 --> 00:03:12.030
at UCLA and came up with a way

00:03:12.030 --> 00:03:14.340
to get their legs moving again.

00:03:14.340 --> 00:03:16.901
So very simple approach at the beginning,

00:03:16.901 --> 00:03:18.690
stimulating the spinal cord electrically,

00:03:18.690 --> 00:03:21.420
the region that normally
control leg muscles,

00:03:21.420 --> 00:03:23.910
anatomically intact after
a spinal cord injury,

00:03:23.910 --> 00:03:25.510
but disconnected from the brain.

00:03:27.540 --> 00:03:28.500
And it was quite amazing

00:03:28.500 --> 00:03:31.080
because as early as you
applied the stimulation,

00:03:31.080 --> 00:03:34.181
the animal would start
stepping automatically

00:03:34.181 --> 00:03:35.580
on the treadmill.

00:03:35.580 --> 00:03:39.330
When I met him, I saw the
the mice and rats walking,

00:03:39.330 --> 00:03:40.890
and it was fantastic.

00:03:40.890 --> 00:03:43.530
The idea was to bring
it to the human being.

00:03:43.530 --> 00:03:45.480
I don't think like the average person

00:03:45.480 --> 00:03:48.630
knows that this is even
possible right now.

00:03:48.630 --> 00:03:50.820
Maybe explain like what you're finding

00:03:50.820 --> 00:03:52.950
people are able to do.

00:03:52.950 --> 00:03:54.990
If you have a spinal cord injury,

00:03:54.990 --> 00:03:57.660
your brain is intact, giving
a command to your legs,

00:03:57.660 --> 00:03:59.100
but this command is interrupted

00:03:59.100 --> 00:04:00.900
at the level of the injury,

00:04:00.900 --> 00:04:02.250
and the idea of the treatment

00:04:02.250 --> 00:04:04.650
is to reactivate the disconnected part

00:04:04.650 --> 00:04:09.179
of the spinal cord in order
to then activate your legs.

00:04:09.179 --> 00:04:10.650
So how do you activate this
part of the spinal cord?

00:04:10.650 --> 00:04:11.673
With electricity.

00:04:12.540 --> 00:04:15.183
So this is what we call the paddle lead.

00:04:16.110 --> 00:04:18.780
It has 16 electrodes as you see here,

00:04:18.780 --> 00:04:20.910
eight and eight in parallel.

00:04:20.910 --> 00:04:23.160
And we implant this paddle lead

00:04:23.160 --> 00:04:26.103
in the dorsal aspect
above the spinal cord.

00:04:28.140 --> 00:04:29.490
The electrodes get implanted

00:04:29.490 --> 00:04:30.930
on a different part of the spine

00:04:30.930 --> 00:04:33.243
depending on what the
patient is trying to do.

00:04:34.200 --> 00:04:35.790
Place them on the lower spine,

00:04:35.790 --> 00:04:37.390
and you can get the legs moving.

00:04:38.280 --> 00:04:40.110
When the first patient
stood up, I don't know,

00:04:40.110 --> 00:04:43.230
in my head I'm picturing
everybody like crying and hugging.

00:04:43.230 --> 00:04:44.310
What was it like?

00:04:44.310 --> 00:04:45.243
Or is it more?

00:04:46.230 --> 00:04:47.190
Yeah, you are in Europe.

00:04:47.190 --> 00:04:48.981
Yeah.

00:04:48.981 --> 00:04:50.430
You have Italian, you
have a lot of emotion,

00:04:50.430 --> 00:04:53.370
so there was a lot of bad words.

00:04:53.370 --> 00:04:54.203
Yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:04:54.203 --> 00:04:56.080
So, but it was a big emotional.

00:04:56.080 --> 00:04:57.837
Yeah, it was like, .

00:04:57.837 --> 00:04:59.850
In other patients,

00:04:59.850 --> 00:05:02.310
the electrodes are placed
higher up on the spine

00:05:02.310 --> 00:05:04.410
to help regulate blood pressure,

00:05:04.410 --> 00:05:07.413
a less obvious but equally
life-changing effect.

00:05:10.350 --> 00:05:11.910
My name is Julie.

00:05:11.910 --> 00:05:15.930
I'm 23 years old, and I'm an archeologist.

00:05:15.930 --> 00:05:19.530
One year and a half ago,
we had a major car accident

00:05:19.530 --> 00:05:24.210
in Serbia and I basically broke my neck.

00:05:24.210 --> 00:05:28.470
My condition of blood
pressure issues got so bad

00:05:28.470 --> 00:05:31.650
that I was basically like
what you guys were looking for

00:05:31.650 --> 00:05:32.483
in the study.

00:05:33.557 --> 00:05:37.177
And then we came here and I got implanted.

00:05:37.177 --> 00:05:38.940
Yeah, I did not know that
blood pressure issues

00:05:38.940 --> 00:05:41.340
are something that's quite common

00:05:41.340 --> 00:05:43.650
with this type of injury, but it is.

00:05:43.650 --> 00:05:46.951
Without, I mean my
blood pressure is so low

00:05:46.951 --> 00:05:49.620
that I'm basically just
stuck in bed all the time.

00:05:49.620 --> 00:05:51.450
Even just sitting up in a wheelchair,

00:05:51.450 --> 00:05:53.493
it's like running a marathon.

00:05:54.470 --> 00:05:55.713
Yeah.

00:05:55.713 --> 00:05:57.510
It also has a huge impact
on my mental state of mind.

00:05:57.510 --> 00:05:58.343
Yeah.

00:05:58.343 --> 00:06:00.570
Without the stimulation,

00:06:00.570 --> 00:06:03.720
there is so little oxygen
circulating in my brain

00:06:03.720 --> 00:06:08.720
that there is not much
joy in life, let's say.

00:06:08.910 --> 00:06:11.610
And then when you turn it on,

00:06:11.610 --> 00:06:15.210
it's like back to some
sort of normal level,

00:06:15.210 --> 00:06:17.910
and it's just the increase of energy,

00:06:17.910 --> 00:06:21.630
being able to breathe and
speak at the same time.

00:06:21.630 --> 00:06:24.750
I'm able to make plans to travel

00:06:24.750 --> 00:06:28.623
to just have a more positive
outlook on life in general.

00:06:31.890 --> 00:06:34.223
Now it's still a little bit of a warmup.

00:06:40.778 --> 00:06:45.778
Michel is a young man who
had a motor bike accident

00:06:46.140 --> 00:06:47.820
a few years ago already

00:06:47.820 --> 00:06:52.236
with a complete motor
and sensitive paralysis.

00:06:52.236 --> 00:06:53.069
Okay.

00:06:53.069 --> 00:06:55.170
And this is a real athlete.

00:06:55.170 --> 00:06:57.060
He trained a lot with us,

00:06:57.060 --> 00:07:00.603
and he's using this technology
every day, many hours.

00:07:02.940 --> 00:07:05.610
I have two buttons, one per side.

00:07:05.610 --> 00:07:10.610
Every time I press, I send
the signal to the tablet,

00:07:11.670 --> 00:07:15.213
and I have the movement, I make the step.

00:07:21.870 --> 00:07:24.780
The walk may be strained,
but it's good enough

00:07:24.780 --> 00:07:26.220
that Michel is mostly able

00:07:26.220 --> 00:07:28.830
to get around without a wheelchair.

00:07:28.830 --> 00:07:32.550
And the implant lets him do
other kinds of exercises too.

00:07:32.550 --> 00:07:34.290
He has a program that just target

00:07:34.290 --> 00:07:36.977
the region of the spinal
cord involving trunk muscle.

00:07:36.977 --> 00:07:39.071
So he is uploading the program.

00:07:39.071 --> 00:07:42.273
Just pressing a button I have a six pack.

00:07:43.110 --> 00:07:44.550
Oh wow.

00:07:44.550 --> 00:07:45.753
I need this program.

00:07:52.890 --> 00:07:54.900
Rather fantastically, Michel,

00:07:54.900 --> 00:07:57.210
whose legs were completely paralyzed,

00:07:57.210 --> 00:07:58.980
has regained a tiny amount

00:07:58.980 --> 00:08:01.830
of homegrown organic muscle control.

00:08:01.830 --> 00:08:04.020
Something in my body is changing.

00:08:04.020 --> 00:08:07.470
I now it is getting some
twitch on the muscles.

00:08:07.470 --> 00:08:08.876
Okay.

00:08:08.876 --> 00:08:09.873
Under the region.

00:08:09.873 --> 00:08:10.706
Yeah.

00:08:10.706 --> 00:08:13.590
We know that the residual nerve fibers,

00:08:13.590 --> 00:08:15.960
cause there are always
some residual nerve fibers,

00:08:15.960 --> 00:08:18.780
they'll grow because of the stimulation

00:08:18.780 --> 00:08:21.450
and that improve the communication

00:08:21.450 --> 00:08:23.500
to the point that they can regain control

00:08:24.568 --> 00:08:26.100
over the paralyzed muscles.

00:08:26.100 --> 00:08:28.290
The doctors don't know
how far the regrowth

00:08:28.290 --> 00:08:30.480
of nerve fibers can go.

00:08:30.480 --> 00:08:33.540
With only a handful of
patients implanted so far,

00:08:33.540 --> 00:08:34.983
there's still much to learn,

00:08:35.880 --> 00:08:38.160
but there are other ways
that Jocelyne and Grégoire

00:08:38.160 --> 00:08:40.560
want to push the tech forward.

00:08:40.560 --> 00:08:43.410
They have started to combine
brain computer interfaces

00:08:43.410 --> 00:08:45.420
with the spinal implants.

00:08:45.420 --> 00:08:47.580
That is to say they're putting electrodes

00:08:47.580 --> 00:08:51.780
in the brain and on the spine
and having them communicate.

00:08:51.780 --> 00:08:54.450
This lets patients simply
think about actions

00:08:54.450 --> 00:08:56.340
like walking or swimming

00:08:56.340 --> 00:08:59.400
and have their bodies
pick up on their desires.

00:08:59.400 --> 00:09:00.233
What do you think?

00:09:00.233 --> 00:09:02.103
You going brain implants?

00:09:04.860 --> 00:09:09.833
I took with my hair.

00:09:11.612 --> 00:09:13.676
Two years ago, Onward went public

00:09:13.676 --> 00:09:15.276
and raised $100 million dollars.

00:09:17.640 --> 00:09:19.380
They're still in the clinical trial phase

00:09:19.380 --> 00:09:20.880
with much of this work,

00:09:20.880 --> 00:09:23.130
but are on track to
become the first company

00:09:23.130 --> 00:09:25.413
to commercialize these kinds of implants.

00:09:26.640 --> 00:09:28.800
The hope is to get them
covered by insurance

00:09:28.800 --> 00:09:31.770
and accessible to the millions
of people around the world

00:09:31.770 --> 00:09:33.990
living with spinal cord injuries.

00:09:33.990 --> 00:09:35.220
Every time I see him progressing,

00:09:35.220 --> 00:09:37.920
each time I see him, it's very rewarding.

00:09:37.920 --> 00:09:39.390
On the other hand, it's very frustrating

00:09:39.390 --> 00:09:42.000
because I would like to see 1000, Michele.

00:09:42.000 --> 00:09:43.692
It's very cool, man.

00:09:43.692 --> 00:09:45.113
I can tell you're very focused.

00:09:46.215 --> 00:09:48.092
Every day, I want even more.

00:09:48.092 --> 00:09:50.009
Every time I want more.

00:09:56.180 --> 00:09:57.847
As Werner Herzog said,

00:09:57.847 --> 00:10:02.677
"The fountain is life
or a tourist attraction.

00:10:02.677 --> 00:10:03.957
"It's totally up to you."

00:10:07.229 --> 00:10:09.960
This is Geneva's fountain hotspot.

00:10:09.960 --> 00:10:13.800
It's beautiful, ejaculatory,

00:10:13.800 --> 00:10:16.160
everything you could want in a fountain.

00:10:18.433 --> 00:10:19.763
Do you want me to do a straighter one?

00:10:21.420 --> 00:10:24.900
I'm not going to lie, I
didn't exactly do my homework

00:10:24.900 --> 00:10:26.763
on the touristy bits of Switzerland.

00:10:27.690 --> 00:10:29.223
Is this Lake Geneva?

00:10:30.210 --> 00:10:31.043
Let's find out.

00:10:32.940 --> 00:10:34.440
I'm not getting a clear answer on that

00:10:34.440 --> 00:10:35.790
but we're going to go with it.

00:10:40.740 --> 00:10:42.483
It's nice as you can see.

00:10:43.320 --> 00:10:46.923
But me, I'm just here for the brains.

00:10:51.873 --> 00:10:53.520
And in cities like Zurich and Lausanne,

00:10:53.520 --> 00:10:57.480
you can find some of the
world's brain research hotspots.

00:10:57.480 --> 00:11:01.650
Here neuroscience and computer
science mix promiscuously

00:11:01.650 --> 00:11:04.590
with wildly creative results.

00:11:04.590 --> 00:11:08.520
For example, this lab full
of rodent twitch streamers

00:11:08.520 --> 00:11:12.420
run by animal research
superstar Mackenzie Mathis.

00:11:12.420 --> 00:11:13.710
So it sounds a little bit far out,

00:11:13.710 --> 00:11:15.990
but we really teach
mice to play video games

00:11:15.990 --> 00:11:18.363
and use little joysticks like pilots.

00:11:19.320 --> 00:11:20.820
But the cool thing about this is,

00:11:20.820 --> 00:11:21.960
is that we then allow to see

00:11:21.960 --> 00:11:24.450
how they learn these different
skills and how they adapt,

00:11:24.450 --> 00:11:27.210
and at the same time can
record the neural activity.

00:11:27.210 --> 00:11:30.014
And we hope to turn sort
of the lessons learned

00:11:30.014 --> 00:11:32.314
in the brain into literally
new AI algorithms.

00:11:33.600 --> 00:11:36.180
What are we going to see today?

00:11:36.180 --> 00:11:38.070
So today we're going to
take you into the lab

00:11:38.070 --> 00:11:41.103
and show you some of the latest
gen video games for mice.

00:11:44.280 --> 00:11:46.560
They're kind of in a freely moving world,

00:11:46.560 --> 00:11:48.360
but all the world is a screen,

00:11:48.360 --> 00:11:51.630
and it changes based on
their real time movements.

00:11:51.630 --> 00:11:54.210
This is the VR world.

00:11:54.210 --> 00:11:55.350
Exactly.

00:11:55.350 --> 00:11:56.610
Are we going to put a mouse in here?

00:11:56.610 --> 00:11:57.893
We're going to put a mouse in here.

00:11:58.733 --> 00:12:00.055
Okay.

00:12:00.055 --> 00:12:01.075
Yeah.

00:12:01.075 --> 00:12:01.908
So if I am not mistaken,

00:12:01.908 --> 00:12:04.290
this is Anchovy and Barracuda.

00:12:04.290 --> 00:12:05.373
So who is that?

00:12:05.373 --> 00:12:07.530
This is Anchovy

00:12:07.530 --> 00:12:08.583
Anchovy is off.

00:12:09.930 --> 00:12:12.630
It's this idea of they're
learning this game.

00:12:12.630 --> 00:12:14.520
They're facing challenges

00:12:14.520 --> 00:12:16.380
or they're going through this environment.

00:12:16.380 --> 00:12:17.737
Yes, exactly.

00:12:17.737 --> 00:12:19.350
And it's just what is the brain doing

00:12:19.350 --> 00:12:22.080
as they try to like negotiate
that and figure things out?

00:12:22.080 --> 00:12:23.013
Yeah, exactly.

00:12:24.210 --> 00:12:25.170
What did she do?

00:12:25.170 --> 00:12:26.280
She made the things appear

00:12:26.280 --> 00:12:28.057
by standing in the middle

00:12:28.057 --> 00:12:30.135
and then facing with the right
heading angle at the screen.

00:12:31.631 --> 00:12:33.960
Anchovy may be impressive,

00:12:33.960 --> 00:12:36.543
but she has yet to enter her final form.

00:12:37.860 --> 00:12:40.740
So in order to be able to
measure the neural activity,

00:12:40.740 --> 00:12:43.320
what we do for part of the experiments

00:12:43.320 --> 00:12:47.073
is to replace the top of the
skull with a glass plate.

00:12:50.070 --> 00:12:52.840
Behold, mouse brains

00:12:54.774 --> 00:12:56.040
This mouse had this surgery,

00:12:56.040 --> 00:12:58.233
and now she's learned to play this game.

00:13:00.472 --> 00:13:03.420
I think the mouse treadmill's so funny.

00:13:03.420 --> 00:13:06.120
The mice themselves are
genetically engineered

00:13:06.120 --> 00:13:08.250
such that in some neurons,

00:13:08.250 --> 00:13:10.440
their neurons actually glow green.

00:13:10.440 --> 00:13:12.660
And then you can capture
this with a camera

00:13:12.660 --> 00:13:15.270
and then we can extract sort
of the regions of interest

00:13:15.270 --> 00:13:17.100
from all these different neurons

00:13:17.100 --> 00:13:18.720
and then analyze them in relation

00:13:18.720 --> 00:13:21.903
to the actual gameplay or the
actual behavior of the animal.

00:13:23.130 --> 00:13:25.770
The ultimate goal is to
turn these mousy thoughts

00:13:25.770 --> 00:13:26.643
into data.

00:13:27.840 --> 00:13:30.030
Mackenzie wants to record
how brains function

00:13:30.030 --> 00:13:34.020
in certain situations and
use those insights to inform

00:13:34.020 --> 00:13:35.883
and shape AI systems.

00:13:37.200 --> 00:13:39.540
In other words, she
wants to use real brains

00:13:39.540 --> 00:13:41.823
to make better artificial brains.

00:13:42.853 --> 00:13:43.686
We hope by trying to understand

00:13:43.686 --> 00:13:45.840
like literally the algorithm
or the computations,

00:13:45.840 --> 00:13:47.370
like are they using arithmetic?

00:13:47.370 --> 00:13:48.873
Is it calculus?

00:13:48.873 --> 00:13:49.706
Like what are they doing?

00:13:49.706 --> 00:13:52.740
And how can we potentially
take those sort of computations

00:13:52.740 --> 00:13:55.620
and then test them in new AI algorithms?

00:13:55.620 --> 00:13:56.970
So that's the general idea.

00:13:59.820 --> 00:14:01.260
Mice are nice and all,

00:14:01.260 --> 00:14:02.520
but they're not the only critters

00:14:02.520 --> 00:14:04.438
having their brain scanned

00:14:04.438 --> 00:14:06.138
here at Swiss Research Institutes.

00:14:07.110 --> 00:14:08.460
These are our fly stocks.

00:14:08.460 --> 00:14:11.430
So each and every single
one of these fly vials

00:14:11.430 --> 00:14:15.243
contains flies that are
genetically distinct.

00:14:19.380 --> 00:14:24.150
Meet Ana Marija Jaksic,
the queen of the flies.

00:14:24.150 --> 00:14:26.400
We normally have to anesthetize them

00:14:26.400 --> 00:14:28.110
because they're flies, they fly.

00:14:28.110 --> 00:14:31.638
So we have to kind of
keep them nice and still.

00:14:31.638 --> 00:14:35.820
Your objective is to find
males versus females.

00:14:35.820 --> 00:14:37.530
Let's do that.

00:14:37.530 --> 00:14:40.500
The male is the dark butt, right?

00:14:40.500 --> 00:14:41.333
Yeah.

00:14:41.333 --> 00:14:42.166
Yeah, that's correct.

00:14:42.166 --> 00:14:42.999
That's right?

00:14:42.999 --> 00:14:44.550
Yeah, yeah, you're a natural.

00:14:46.680 --> 00:14:49.620
So our basic question in our lab is

00:14:49.620 --> 00:14:50.730
in complex environments

00:14:50.730 --> 00:14:52.260
you have to have to
learn where things are.

00:14:52.260 --> 00:14:54.060
You have to memorize things.

00:14:54.060 --> 00:14:57.000
So we're trying to kind of
recreate these situations

00:14:57.000 --> 00:15:00.320
for the flies, and we try to
evolve cognition in flies.

00:15:00.320 --> 00:15:02.040
So you're trying to
make super smart flies.

00:15:02.040 --> 00:15:03.570
Yeah, exactly.

00:15:03.570 --> 00:15:05.160
Fly masterminds.

00:15:05.160 --> 00:15:07.359
What could go wrong?

00:15:07.359 --> 00:15:08.192
Something like this, yeah.

00:15:08.192 --> 00:15:10.080
So you breed smarter and smarter flies.

00:15:10.080 --> 00:15:11.010
You get them to do these tasks.

00:15:11.010 --> 00:15:12.601
Exactly.

00:15:12.601 --> 00:15:13.680
You find which ones perform the best.

00:15:13.680 --> 00:15:15.398
Take those, breed them again.

00:15:15.398 --> 00:15:16.231
Exactly.

00:15:18.709 --> 00:15:21.033
It's basically fly eugenics.

00:15:22.050 --> 00:15:23.490
But how do you separate

00:15:23.490 --> 00:15:26.610
the Fleinsteins from the Flimbeciles?

00:15:26.610 --> 00:15:28.713
With a fly-Q test, of course.

00:15:31.980 --> 00:15:36.980
So right now we are conditioning
flies to pick one color

00:15:37.020 --> 00:15:40.620
in a set of two or three colors.

00:15:40.620 --> 00:15:43.620
They're picking between green or blue.

00:15:43.620 --> 00:15:46.230
Every single fly at the
very end of the experiment

00:15:46.230 --> 00:15:48.690
will get a a learning score.

00:15:48.690 --> 00:15:52.200
We get rid of the 10%
of the worst performers,

00:15:52.200 --> 00:15:55.575
and we take the majority of the flies, so.

00:15:55.575 --> 00:15:56.780
I see, okay.

00:15:56.780 --> 00:16:00.210
As she breeds generation
after generation of bugs,

00:16:00.210 --> 00:16:02.910
Ana Marija hopes to learn
about the relationship

00:16:02.910 --> 00:16:05.670
between genes and intelligence.

00:16:05.670 --> 00:16:07.350
We can track specifically

00:16:07.350 --> 00:16:09.150
what are the genes that are being selected

00:16:09.150 --> 00:16:10.590
for cognitive behaviors.

00:16:10.590 --> 00:16:13.470
How specifically does
the brain actually change

00:16:13.470 --> 00:16:14.820
in its structure?

00:16:14.820 --> 00:16:18.360
And all of this we can
actually use for wet wear.

00:16:18.360 --> 00:16:21.660
So instead of using physical chips,

00:16:21.660 --> 00:16:24.960
we can use biological
chips like neurons itself

00:16:24.960 --> 00:16:27.793
to actually power our computers someday.

00:16:27.793 --> 00:16:28.796
I see, yeah.

00:16:28.796 --> 00:16:29.930
In the future, you know?

00:16:29.930 --> 00:16:31.212
Yeah, I think every scientist

00:16:31.212 --> 00:16:32.100
I've interviewed for this show so far

00:16:32.100 --> 00:16:35.452
just about is like trying
to make AI smarter.

00:16:35.452 --> 00:16:36.492
Yeah, yeah.

00:16:36.492 --> 00:16:37.325
And better.

00:16:37.325 --> 00:16:38.730
It's the ultimate kind of goal

00:16:38.730 --> 00:16:41.280
to see how to produce ourselves, right?

00:16:41.280 --> 00:16:42.113
Yeah.

00:16:47.310 --> 00:16:50.490
In many ways, even the
tiniest corporeal brains

00:16:50.490 --> 00:16:53.040
are smarter than our best AIs.

00:16:53.040 --> 00:16:54.570
So the researchers here think

00:16:54.570 --> 00:16:57.090
that studying these
creatures is our best shot

00:16:57.090 --> 00:17:00.510
at creating things like
highly capable robots.

00:17:00.510 --> 00:17:02.850
If we could build an AI that
could do what a mouse does,

00:17:02.850 --> 00:17:05.730
I think that would be a
huge feat for humanity.

00:17:05.730 --> 00:17:08.130
Maybe within 10 years
we could have, you know,

00:17:08.130 --> 00:17:09.150
mini mice robots.

00:17:09.150 --> 00:17:12.420
Not that we want that for
humanity, but conceptually, right?

00:17:12.420 --> 00:17:14.012
We want something like an embodied agent

00:17:14.012 --> 00:17:15.540
that can act in the world

00:17:15.540 --> 00:17:17.730
and very adaptively change its behavior.

00:17:17.730 --> 00:17:21.735
I just pictured like New
York full of robotic AI mice

00:17:21.735 --> 00:17:22.590
and was horrified.

00:17:22.590 --> 00:17:24.040
That would be pretty scary.

00:17:26.029 --> 00:17:29.580
Of course, the brain I want
most to understand is my own.

00:17:29.580 --> 00:17:32.753
So I decided to volunteer
as a test subject.

00:17:42.270 --> 00:17:45.963
Okay, friends ready to
go down the rabbit hole?

00:17:51.690 --> 00:17:54.750
This lab at the Swiss Federal
Institute of Technology

00:17:54.750 --> 00:17:58.563
is run by an eccentric
psychonaut named Olaf Blanke.

00:17:59.820 --> 00:18:01.560
He invited me to try an experiment

00:18:01.560 --> 00:18:04.863
that's meant to induce the
feeling of a ghostly presence.

00:18:06.090 --> 00:18:08.640
Real talk, I'm a little bit scared,

00:18:08.640 --> 00:18:11.853
but first let's get existential with Olaf.

00:18:14.070 --> 00:18:17.340
So I've been interested
for a long time in the self

00:18:17.340 --> 00:18:19.530
and how it is related to our body,

00:18:19.530 --> 00:18:21.630
how the brain actually
represents our body.

00:18:21.630 --> 00:18:23.727
How are my hands represented in my body?

00:18:23.727 --> 00:18:25.893
How is your brain representing your body?

00:18:28.440 --> 00:18:30.750
What gives us the conscious
experience of being someone?

00:18:30.750 --> 00:18:32.340
Of being a person?

00:18:32.340 --> 00:18:34.530
We are looking at
something very perceptual.

00:18:34.530 --> 00:18:36.540
We call it a minimal self.

00:18:36.540 --> 00:18:39.630
And we think that this minimal
self is crucial for feeling

00:18:39.630 --> 00:18:41.313
that I'm feeling right now
sitting in front of you

00:18:41.313 --> 00:18:42.660
here talking to you.

00:18:42.660 --> 00:18:45.840
So this very like basic
fundamental connection

00:18:45.840 --> 00:18:47.310
between mind and and body.

00:18:47.310 --> 00:18:49.470
Exactly, so it's an active brain process.

00:18:49.470 --> 00:18:50.303
Yeah.

00:18:50.303 --> 00:18:51.840
On a millisecond by millisecond basis,

00:18:51.840 --> 00:18:53.730
we have to generate this representation

00:18:53.730 --> 00:18:57.180
where we are in space,
and it better be here

00:18:57.180 --> 00:18:58.020
where my body is located.

00:18:58.020 --> 00:18:59.160
We take this for granted,

00:18:59.160 --> 00:19:01.428
but this process is
happening all the time.

00:19:01.428 --> 00:19:02.640
Yes, I think much of.

00:19:02.640 --> 00:19:04.540
Well, except when it isn't.

00:19:09.510 --> 00:19:11.700
Think out-of-body experiences

00:19:11.700 --> 00:19:13.953
or feeling a ghostly presence in the room.

00:19:15.390 --> 00:19:17.520
Anomalies that Olaf first encountered

00:19:17.520 --> 00:19:21.120
while treating patients
with neurological disorders.

00:19:21.120 --> 00:19:22.620
They were talking, for example,

00:19:22.620 --> 00:19:24.960
that before they have an epileptic seizure

00:19:24.960 --> 00:19:26.610
they may have an out of body experience.

00:19:26.610 --> 00:19:27.510
Okay.

00:19:27.510 --> 00:19:29.070
And of course they didn't use that term,

00:19:29.070 --> 00:19:31.350
but they were saying, yeah, listen, Olaf.

00:19:31.350 --> 00:19:32.550
It was strange.

00:19:32.550 --> 00:19:35.820
I was, I had the sensations
for about a couple of seconds

00:19:35.820 --> 00:19:37.710
that I was above myself

00:19:37.710 --> 00:19:39.540
or it could have been a
presence hallucination

00:19:39.540 --> 00:19:41.460
or a ghost sensation.

00:19:41.460 --> 00:19:43.440
I remember one patient in
particular who told me,

00:19:43.440 --> 00:19:46.530
well, there's somebody just
at arm length behind me.

00:19:46.530 --> 00:19:49.740
And whenever she got up and
moved, that person moved along.

00:19:49.740 --> 00:19:51.314
Okay.

00:19:51.314 --> 00:19:52.147
So very strange.

00:19:52.147 --> 00:19:56.009
So is your theory that
stories of ghosts over time,

00:19:56.009 --> 00:19:58.650
this is kind of where it all comes from

00:19:58.650 --> 00:20:00.430
is people having these feelings?

00:20:00.430 --> 00:20:01.486
Yeah, so,

00:20:01.486 --> 00:20:04.103
if you want a naive, quick
response, yes, that's what it is.

00:20:05.070 --> 00:20:06.570
It turns out these sensations

00:20:06.570 --> 00:20:09.213
are a common symptom
of Parkinson's disease.

00:20:11.430 --> 00:20:12.900
So the presence hallucination,

00:20:12.900 --> 00:20:14.670
feeling somebody behind
you that is not there,

00:20:14.670 --> 00:20:17.400
that you can't hear, that you can't see,

00:20:17.400 --> 00:20:19.510
50% of Parkinson patients will have that

00:20:21.628 --> 00:20:23.880
Naturally, this inspired Olaf

00:20:23.880 --> 00:20:26.280
to figure out how to
induce these hallucinations

00:20:26.280 --> 00:20:29.790
on demand using VR and robotics.

00:20:29.790 --> 00:20:31.590
So you will be sitting on this chair,

00:20:31.590 --> 00:20:34.140
and your main task will
consist in moving this robot.

00:20:34.140 --> 00:20:34.973
Okay.

00:20:38.460 --> 00:20:39.970
Can you confirm

00:20:39.970 --> 00:20:41.640
you're in a dark environment right now?

00:20:41.640 --> 00:20:44.070
Yeah, it's, yeah, nothing it's just black.

00:20:44.070 --> 00:20:45.900
Oh, there you are.

00:20:45.900 --> 00:20:48.840
You can see that he is
immersed inside a virtual room

00:20:48.840 --> 00:20:52.080
which is mimicking this
real experimental room.

00:20:52.080 --> 00:20:54.333
So it's a recreation of the room in 3D.

00:20:57.420 --> 00:21:00.390
As I move my hand to
poke a virtual avatar,

00:21:00.390 --> 00:21:04.080
a robot behind me gave
me a corresponding poke.

00:21:04.080 --> 00:21:06.570
It's all meant to confuse
your brain so much

00:21:06.570 --> 00:21:10.050
that you start to sense the
presence of another being

00:21:10.050 --> 00:21:13.233
while Olaf studies what's
going on in your brain.

00:21:16.545 --> 00:21:17.986
Thank you.

00:21:17.986 --> 00:21:19.080
Okay, perfect.

00:21:19.080 --> 00:21:20.280
That was weird.

00:21:20.280 --> 00:21:21.810
Sometimes I sort of
thought I was doing it,

00:21:21.810 --> 00:21:24.750
and then sometimes it really
felt like almost like an animal

00:21:24.750 --> 00:21:27.363
or something was kind of kneading in my.

00:21:28.687 --> 00:21:31.200
The sensation for me
was not all that strong,

00:21:31.200 --> 00:21:32.733
but that's partly the point.

00:21:39.600 --> 00:21:40.920
Since Parkinson's patients

00:21:40.920 --> 00:21:43.650
are more susceptible to
the illusion than others,

00:21:43.650 --> 00:21:45.030
the experiment could function

00:21:45.030 --> 00:21:48.300
as a kind of early warning
system for the disease.

00:21:48.300 --> 00:21:49.830
With a robotic device,

00:21:49.830 --> 00:21:51.210
we don't need to wait for the symptoms.

00:21:51.210 --> 00:21:53.760
We don't need to wait for the
patient to have Parkinson's,

00:21:53.760 --> 00:21:56.460
but we could expose people
to this much earlier

00:21:56.460 --> 00:21:58.350
if they have other risk factors.

00:21:58.350 --> 00:22:01.770
And if they would be part of
this very elevated sensitivity,

00:22:01.770 --> 00:22:03.720
we could team up, for
example, with companies

00:22:03.720 --> 00:22:05.970
starting very early
neuroprotective therapies

00:22:05.970 --> 00:22:08.280
trying to delay the onset of Parkinson's

00:22:08.280 --> 00:22:09.333
or cure Parkinson's.

00:22:13.920 --> 00:22:17.400
What an exciting time between VR,

00:22:17.400 --> 00:22:20.070
brain computer interface,
artificial intelligence.

00:22:20.070 --> 00:22:23.072
I mean, it must be like exhilarating

00:22:23.072 --> 00:22:24.540
to have this intersection.

00:22:24.540 --> 00:22:26.010
Sure, no, so no, it's absolutely,

00:22:26.010 --> 00:22:28.830
so I think what is
special about this time,

00:22:28.830 --> 00:22:31.860
I think, when I started
neuroscience was neuroscience.

00:22:31.860 --> 00:22:33.690
You know, philosophy was philosophy,

00:22:33.690 --> 00:22:35.760
and certainly engineering was engineering.

00:22:35.760 --> 00:22:39.270
There was always an interest
in medical applications.

00:22:39.270 --> 00:22:41.360
But what is really amazing happening now

00:22:41.360 --> 00:22:42.960
is it doesn't matter anymore, right?

00:22:42.960 --> 00:22:44.272
Yeah.

00:22:44.272 --> 00:22:45.275
We're all working on similar problems

00:22:45.275 --> 00:22:47.493
and issues and want to resolve this.

00:22:49.570 --> 00:22:52.620
The brain is, I think, by definition

00:22:52.620 --> 00:22:54.900
something that's fascinating for everyone,

00:22:54.900 --> 00:22:56.820
and it needs expertise
from all these domains,

00:22:56.820 --> 00:22:58.140
I think to have even a chance

00:22:58.140 --> 00:22:59.740
to understand small parts of it.

00:23:03.660 --> 00:23:06.540
80 years after Hoffman's psychedelic ride

00:23:06.540 --> 00:23:10.358
Switzerland's brain research
complex is still going strong,

00:23:10.358 --> 00:23:14.103
but the mysteries of the
mind remain mysterious.

00:23:15.240 --> 00:23:16.410
We still don't really know

00:23:16.410 --> 00:23:18.940
how billions of neurons
firing creates a thought

00:23:20.100 --> 00:23:22.500
or how to fully restore
a broken connection

00:23:22.500 --> 00:23:23.913
between mind and body.

00:23:25.290 --> 00:23:27.060
Researchers here will be on this quest

00:23:27.060 --> 00:23:31.320
for a long time to come, but
it's a journey worth taking,

00:23:31.320 --> 00:23:33.423
and I'm excited to see what's next.

00:23:35.070 --> 00:23:37.470
That is, as long as it's not AI rodents

00:23:37.470 --> 00:23:38.733
bent on our destruction.

